[{:seon.task/name "files-namespaces-and-symbols-are-plain-markdown"
  :seon.task/plan "Done; what exists. Files, namespaces and symbols are plain Markdown, read by the one reader (DECISIONS: files, namespaces and symbols are plain Markdown, read by the one reader). `seon.ref/span-candidate` keeps a backticked dotted name that names a namespace (v1's rule, `~/src/seon/src/seon/ref.clj` `span-candidate`), and `seon.ref/namespace-view` shows it at its depth (summary its docstring's first sentence, doc its docstring and publics). A cited file outside the stored wiki (`bin/root`, `src/seon/db.clj`, `reference-code/<project>/README.md#x`) is read on demand by `seon.ref/file-view`, never stored (`seon.docs` stores only `docs/`), through the one `seon.ref/shown` rule; a `reference-code/` citation records the submodule's pinned revision from git, as v1's `:seon.ref/pin`. All three kinds reach the context's `:sections` through `seon.context/cited-view`. `:seon.ref/name` accepts a simple symbol; a `path:N` citation is not shown; a non-Markdown file at summary or doc depth shows its address and line count only. Rule: docs/references.md#files-and-namespaces-on-demand."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/a-namespace-is-read-as-a-namespace
                      seon.context-test/a-cited-file-is-read-on-demand}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-summary-says-there-is-more"
  :seon.task/plan "A cited section at :summary depth shows its lead sentence (`seon.ref/shown`) and says nothing of the rest. Correct: the summary view says how much it left and the citation that reads it at :doc depth (AGENTS §9: every cut says what it left and how to read the rest). Replaces nothing; the summary view gains one line."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/a-summary-names-the-citation-that-reads-the-rest}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-parser-reads-with-options-built-once"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.ref/parse` reads each candidate name with `seon.reader/read-text`, which rebuilds `seon.reader/opts` on every call: about half of the ~13 us and 18 KB a name costs (docs/references.md#cost). Correct: the options are one value built where `seon.reader` defines them, and `read-text` reads with it; a pure speedup of the one parser, so first of the string-parser tasks. Not a cache (the value has no inputs, so nothing is keyed by time or anything else). Deletes the per-call construction. Proof: `seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass` keeps its bound and states the lower per-name bytes it now measures (work done: bytes allocated and contracted calls per candidate, never time); every `seon.reader-test` law stays green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass seon.reader-test/the-laws-hold-over-generated-replies}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outgoing-text-cites-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner wants one string parser run through what Seon says and reads, to pull in relevant context (2026-10-23). What exists: `seon.ref/parse` runs only on authored prose (a task's plan, a wiki page, a docstring); an eval item's :printed, an error's text and a tool's output are never parsed, so an agent that sees `at seon.agent/error-map 3` or a stack frame must ask `(source seon.agent/error-map)` in a turn of its own. Measured 2026-10-23 on four error-shaped strings: parse already reads var names, keywords, `path#anchor` and `path:N` out of them (resolve-only, summary depth), and a four-space-indented stack frame (`    seon.agent/g (REPL:7)`) is read like any line, so no separate flat mode is needed for names (the log-line hypothesis did not reproduce; a position inside `(REPL:7)` is read as no citation, which `a-citation-can-carry-a-position` is about). Correct: at the one place an item's text is made (the REPL's :printed, `seon.repl/wrap-caught` and its print middleware), run `seon.ref/parse` over that text and resolve its citations on the agent's db with `seon.ref/references`, the same resolution a task's plan gets, and show each hit at summary depth by `seon.ref/shown` under the existing budget (`:seon.config.render/reference-tokens`; a new key, if one is needed, goes in default.edn). It reuses the one parser and the one resolver: no second grammar, no per-kind code (the error's own :seon.error/in and :seon.error/at stay data, this reads unstructured text). A name that resolves to nothing is silent here (resolve-only), never an error. Replaces: the agent's follow-up `(source ..)` and `(doc ..)` turns for what the text already names; measured as turns to green on the e2e tasks before and after. Cost proof (work done, never time): per outgoing text, the contracted calls and bytes are linear in its characters and its candidate names, plus one index seek per resolved name; n = 100 names, and a text with no names costs one scan. Proofs: an item whose text names a var cites it; an unresolved name adds nothing; the cost proof. Wiki: references.md (a new section) and render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-item-cites-the-vars-its-text-names seon.ref-test/parsing-an-outgoing-text-is-linear-in-its-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-citation-can-carry-a-position"
  :seon.task/plan "WAITS FOR OWNER RULING: new syntax (Root's gate #4); do not build before it. Idea: `at ns/f 3:6` reads today as the bare var `ns/f` and the position is dropped. Let a citation of a var carry the form-relative line and column that `seon.error/at` already states (`(source v)` coordinates, 1-based) so context can show the var's recorded source with that line marked. Gate answers to bring the owner: (1) which existing mechanism does this: none, `path:N` is files only and marked stale, `seon.ref` has no position in a name; (2) v1: none; (3) the field: stack-frame `file:line` and compiler `file:line:col` are universal, but ours are form-relative on purpose; (4) why the error's own data cannot do it: it can for errors (`:seon.error/in`, `:seon.error/at`), this task is for text that is not an error map (a log, a plan, an agent's note). Plan if approved: one rule in `seon.ref/scan` beside `path:N`, a citation key for the position, a view that marks the line; positions stay out of stored facts (nothing stored depends on line numbers). Proof: `seon.ref-test/a-var-with-a-position-shows-its-line`. Deletes nothing; do not add it if only error maps carry positions."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ref/an-outgoing-text-cites-what-it-names"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/a-var-with-a-position-shows-its-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-alias-is-resolved-in-its-namespace"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a text's `str/join` is read as a resolve-only candidate that names nothing (`seon.ref/names-something?`), because an alias is looked up nowhere; measured 2026-10-23 (it came out of an error string as a candidate and would resolve to no var). The text of an eval item is written in a namespace, whose aliases (`ns-aliases`) say what `str` is. Correct: `seon.ref/parse` and `references` take the namespace the text is read in (nil for a text with none, today's behaviour) and resolve an alias through it before the `:var/name` lookup. One resolver, one more input. Proof: `seon.ref-test/an-alias-names-its-var-in-its-namespace` (`str/join` in a namespace aliasing clojure.string cites `clojure.string/join`; in one that does not, nothing) and the cost proof stays at one seek per name. Deletes nothing; it replaces the agent spelling out full names to be understood."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/an-alias-names-its-var-in-its-namespace seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass}}]
